course: world
lesson: 13
title: Evaporation and condensation
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Water does not need to boil to become a gas: at any temperature some particles at the surface move fast enough to escape into the air. This is evaporation.
explain: Evaporation is faster when it is warm, when it is windy, when the air is dry, and when the water is spread over a large surface.
explain: When water vapour in the air touches something cold, it cools, slows down and turns back into tiny drops of liquid water. This is condensation.
explain: For example, wet washing dries on a line on a warm windy day, and a bathroom mirror mists up during a hot shower.
explain: The white cloud just above a boiling kettle is not the gas itself: it is tiny drops of water that formed when the invisible vapour cooled in the air.
fact: Evaporation is a liquid turning into a gas at its surface, and it happens below the boiling point.
fact: Warmth, wind, dry air and a large surface all make evaporation faster.
fact: Evaporation cools what is left behind, because the fastest particles leave.
fact: Condensation is a gas turning into a liquid when it cools.
fact: Water vapour itself is invisible; clouds, mist, fog near the ground and the white "steam" from a kettle are tiny drops of water.
fact: Dew forms when water vapour in the air condenses on cool grass at night.
fact: Sweat cools our body as it evaporates from the skin.
fact: When salty water evaporates, the water leaves and the salt stays behind.
exercise: Why does a puddle disappear on a sunny day without ever boiling? | The water slowly evaporates into the air | The ground drinks up every single drop of it | The hot Sun burns the water away like a fire | The water turns into fresh air and is gone
exercise: Which shirt will dry fastest? | One hung out on a warm windy day | One hung out on a cold still day | One left folded in a basket | One hung up in a damp cellar
exercise: Why does a bathroom mirror mist up during a hot shower? | Water vapour condenses on the cool mirror | Water seeps out from inside the mirror | Soap bubbles stick to the glass | The hot air makes the glass go soft and cloudy
exercise: What is the white cloud just above a boiling kettle? | Tiny drops of water formed as the vapour cools | Pure invisible water vapour | Smoke from the hot metal | Air that has been heated
exercise: Why do we feel cool when sweat dries on our skin? | Evaporation carries heat away from the skin | Sweat is cold when it comes out | Drying skin lets in cold air | The salt in sweat freezes the skin a little bit
exercise: Where does the dew on grass in the morning come from? | Water vapour in the air condensing on cool grass | Rain that fell quietly in the night | Water from the soil splashing up | Water pulled up from the river nearby during the night
exercise: How should you spread a spilt glass of water so it dries fastest? | Spread it thin over a big area | Collect it into one deep puddle | Cover it with a bowl | Pour it into a narrow jar
exercise: How is evaporation different from boiling? | Evaporation happens at the surface at any temperature | Evaporation happens only at 100 °C, exactly like boiling | Boiling happens only at the surface | There is no difference at all
exercise: Seawater in a shallow pan is left in the Sun until it is dry. What is left? | Salt | Nothing at all | A little fresh water | Only sand
exercise: Why does a can of cold juice get wet on the outside on a warm day? | Water vapour from the air condenses on it | Juice leaks out through the metal | The can sweats like skin does | The cold makes the metal of the can melt a little
exercise: Why do wet clothes dry slowly on a damp day, even when it is not raining? | The air already holds a lot of water vapour | The clouds stop all evaporation | Cold air has no room for particles | The clothes soak up water from the clouds
exercise: What is condensation? | A gas turning into a liquid as it cools | A liquid turning into a gas as it is warmed | A solid turning into a liquid as it warms up | A liquid turning into a solid as it gets cold
exercise: Why do your glasses mist up when you walk into a warm kitchen from the cold? | Water vapour in the warm air condenses on the cold lenses | Dust from outside sticks to the lenses | Cold glass lets out its own water | The heat bends the lenses out of shape and makes them cloudy
exercise: Can you see water vapour? | No, water vapour is an invisible gas | Yes, it is white and fluffy like a cloud | Yes, it is grey and thick like the smoke | Only at night, when it glows a little bit
test: Two equal saucers of water are left out: one on a warm sunny windowsill, one in a cool cupboard. What happens? | The one in the window dries first, because warmth speeds evaporation | The one in the cupboard dries first, because darkness draws water out | Both dry at the same time, because they hold the same water | Neither dries, because water only leaves when it boils
test: Why does breathing on a cold window make a foggy patch? | The vapour in your breath condenses on the cold glass | Your breath carries dust that sticks to the glass | Your breath turns into ice crystals at once | Carbon dioxide turns white on the glass
test: A dog pants with its tongue out on a hot day. How does this help it? | Water evaporating from its tongue cools it | It breathes out its heat as smoke | It swallows cool air into its stomach | It slows its heart down
test: Which is true? | Water can evaporate even from a cold puddle | Water can only evaporate when it boils | Water can only evaporate in sunlight | Evaporation makes the water left behind warmer
test: A lid is put on a pot of hot soup. Later drops of water hang under the lid. Where did they come from? | Vapour from the soup condensed on the cooler lid | The lid was still wet from washing | Water came through the metal from outside | The kitchen air made a little rain right under the lid
test: Which of these shows condensation? | Drops forming on a cold bottle just out of the fridge | A puddle drying in the sunshine | Ice melting in a drink | Sugar disappearing in tea
test: Why does a swimmer feel cold climbing out of the pool on a windy day? | Wind speeds up the evaporation that cools the skin | The pool water is colder than the air | Wind blows cold drops of rain onto the swimmer's skin | Wet skin cannot make any heat
test: A wet floor dries faster after it is mopped thin than when it is left in puddles. Why? | A thin layer has a bigger surface for evaporation | Mopping makes the water colder | The mop soaks up every drop | Puddles are made of heavier water
test: What is fog? | Tiny drops of water formed when vapour cools near the ground | A cloud of smoke from chimneys | Pure water vapour gas | Dust lifted by the wind
test: how come the puddle's gone after it got sunny | The water evaporated into the air | It soaked into the Sun's rays | It froze and blew away | The ground drank it up and stays wet
test: wots that white steam from the kettle actually | Tiny drops of water from cooled vapour | Pure invisible water vapour, nothing else | Smoke coming off the hot metal of the kettle | Air that's been boiled till it goes white
test: Leo says sweat is just the body getting rid of water and doesn't cool u at all. true? | No, sweat cools you as it evaporates from your skin | Yes, sweat has nothing to do with cooling | Yes, sweat makes you warmer | No, sweat only cools you down when it's cold outside
test: Tom reckons the water on the grass in the morning is rain from the night. right? | No, it's dew, vapour that condensed on the cool grass | Yes, it always rains a little at night | Yes, it falls from the clouds as tiny drops | No, it comes up out of the soil
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises evaporation? | A liquid becomes a gas at its surface below the boiling point | A gas becomes a liquid upon cooling | A solid becomes a liquid upon heating | A liquid becomes a gas solely upon reaching the boiling point, never below it
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the white cloud issuing from a kettle. | It consists of minute drops of liquid water | It consists of invisible water vapour | It consists of smoke from the heating element | It consists of air expelled from the water
exam: Identify the principal reason for which perspiration cools the human body. | Its evaporation removes heat from the skin | It is itself colder than the surrounding air | It reflects the heat of the Sun | It prevents blood from reaching the skin
exam: It is given that a dish of salt water is permitted to evaporate entirely. Ascertain what remains. | The salt alone | Nothing whatsoever | The water alone | A diminished quantity of salt water
